    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I have notes from three discovery interviews: the CEO, CFO, and VP Operations at a mid-market PE-backed manufacturer. Read the Discovery Notes file in this workspace. Pull out the three to five strongest themes that cut across all three interviews. For each theme, note which interviewees support it and flag where the perspectives diverge. I need this for a synthesis section of my findings outline.",
      "timestamp": "2026-05-23T09:30:00.000Z"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here are the themes that cut across the three interviews, with supporting voices and where the perspectives diverge.\n\n**Theme 1: Fear of customer concentration is suppressing commercial action.**\nAll three interviewees acknowledge the concentration risk, but in different ways. Andrea named it directly: \"If we lose one of them, we lose 20% of the business overnight.\" David surfaced it quantitatively by pulling the margin split without being asked. Marcus named it operationally: the legacy accounts are easier to run, which makes them feel valuable even when they are not the most profitable. The divergence is in what they think should be done. Andrea seems ready to explore repricing but has not acted. David has the data that points toward customer mix shift but has not been the one to push it. Marcus's instinct runs in the opposite direction: he prefers the legacy accounts for operational simplicity. This is a misaligned incentive worth naming in the deck.\n\n**Theme 2: The business has no real outbound commercial function.**\nAndrea said the phone rang and they answered it for four years. David's 2024 hiring story suggests they tried to build capacity without a plan. Neither the CEO nor CFO mentioned a prospecting process, a target account list, or a lead generation motion. Marcus does not think about customers commercially at all. This theme has no dissenting voice; the three interviews converge on a gap.\n\n**Theme 3: Knowledge concentration in operations is a structural risk.**\nMarcus is the single point of failure on scheduling, throughput knowledge, and the relationship with at least one critical supplier. Andrea praised him warmly but the praise itself signals dependence. David did not mention it, which suggests it is either below his visibility or he is being careful. Worth exploring: has the PE sponsor flagged this as a risk? Single-person knowledge concentration in operations is exactly the kind of thing that shows up in diligence and compresses exit multiples.\n\n**Theme 4: The 2024 headcount additions have not paid off, and no one has addressed it yet.**\nDavid named this carefully; Andrea did not mention the hires at all. Marcus did not come up. This is a sensitive topic: the people are still employed, the investment is recent, and there is no clear owner of the conversation about whether it is working. It is also a real data point: if you can show that those two FTEs represent 1.5 to 2 points of EBITDA margin with no corresponding revenue lift, it becomes a concrete recommendation rather than a personnel observation.\n\n**Theme 5: Margin improvement from new customers is real but has an operational cost Marcus has not quantified.**\nDavid's number (9.8% vs. 18.4% gross margin, legacy vs. new) is the clearest financial signal in the discovery set. But Marcus's observation that the new customers are harder to run is not wrong. The question is whether the operational cost of the higher-complexity setups erodes the gross margin advantage, and by how much. This needs a rough calculation before you can confidently recommend customer mix shift as a margin lever. If the changeover cost per unit on the new accounts is high enough, the 18.4% gross margin may look better than the blended contribution after production overhead.\n\nThe themes that are cleanest for a findings deck are 1, 2, and 3. Themes 4 and 5 need one more data point each before they are recommendation-ready.",
      "timestamp": "2026-05-23T09:34:00.000Z"
    }
  ]
}
